\n[asy]\nunitsize (0.1 cm);\n\ndraw((16,-20)--(-3,-20)--(0,0)--(-3,20)--(16,20));\ndraw((0,0)--(16,0));\ndraw((5,20)--(8,0)--(5,-20));\ndraw((13,20)--(16,0)--(13,-20));\ndot((18,0));\ndot((20,0));\ndot((22,0));\ndraw((24,0)--(50,0));\ndraw((23,20)--(47,20)--(50,0)--(47,-20)--(21,-20));\ndraw((23,20)--(26,0)--(23,-20));\ndraw((31,20)--(34,0)--(31,-20));\ndraw((39,20)--(42,0)--(39,-20));\ndraw((39,21)--(39,25));\ndraw((47,21)--(47,25));\ndraw((39,23)--(47,23));\nlabel(\"$A$\",(-3,20),NW);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(47,20),NE);\nlabel(\"$C$\",(50,0),E);\nlabel(\"$D$\",(47,-20),SE);\nlabel(\"$E$\",(-3,-20),SW);\nlabel(\"$F$\",(0,0),W);\nlabel(\"20'\",(43,23),N);\nlabel(\"62'\",(49,10),E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"7a34714dd2e3263ab8cafce5a5896ace6c470b2b428e577071a5eb4945cbf613"},"seconds":7.414038181304932,"seed":957707484489228031,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the perimeter of hexagon \\(ABCDEF\\), we need to analyze the structure of the chevron pattern formed by the parallelograms. Each parallelogram has sides of 62 feet and 20 feet. The hexagon is formed by the outer edges of these parallelograms.\n\nFirst, let's identify the lengths of the sides of the hexagon. The hexagon \\(ABCDEF\\) is composed of the outer edges of the parallelograms. We can see that the hexagon is made up of 6 sides, and each side is the sum of two sides of the parallelograms, but only one of each type (either 62 feet or 20 feet) is visible in the outer perimeter.\n\nThe hexagon can be divided into two parts: the top and bottom rows of parallelograms, and the middle row. The top and bottom rows each contribute a side of 62 feet, and the middle row contributes sides of 20 feet. Therefore, the perimeter of the hexagon is the sum of these contributions.\n\nThe perimeter \\(P\\) of the hexagon is:\n\\[ P = 2 \\times 62 + 4 \\times 20 = 124 + 80 = 204 \\text{ feet} \\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of hexagon \\(ABCDEF\\) is \\(\\boxed{204}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{204}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:145"}
